                <pb facs="0267" n="131" sameAs="frus:frus1929v01/pg131"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15a3/18: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Great
                        Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Dawes</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-06-24T13:00:00-04:00">June 24, 1929—1
                    p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>154. Your No. 166, June 22, 1 p.m. I agree with your view that it would be wise
                    to keep the Japanese Ambassador closely informed with regard to the subject
                    matter of any conferences which you and Gibson may have with the British, but I
                    do not think that he should be present personally in any such conversations as
                    otherwise the rumor might arise that a conference was already under way to the
                    exclusion of France and Italy.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
